#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Trigonometric Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "5d78daa9-ba7a-554d-83f7-f8354acb9976" :question/math-academy-id "e-5372" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\cos \\theta, \\, y = 2 \\sin \\theta, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ intersects the line $2x-y=0$." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos θ - 2\\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos θ &= 2\\sin θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin θ \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} \\\\\n1 &= \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nso our first solution is\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to $\\theta_1$:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "dc97ffbb-04ca-5b07-ba25-11042f15c8ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-5373" :question/problem "Find the points where the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1257/Source/Images/157c9e3533adbdf48403abc3e6cb25b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t$. Upon finding $t$, we then proceed to find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4(t^{2}) - 2(2t) - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4t^{2} - 4t - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n(2t - 5)(2t + 3) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t$, we proceed to find $x$ and $y$:\n\n- We start with $t=\\dfrac{5}{2}$. For $x$, we have\n$x|= t^{2}; x|= (\\frac{5}{2})^{2}; = \\frac{25}{4}$.\nFor $y$, we have\n$y|= 2t; = 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2}; = 5$.\nSo the coordinates of the first intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)$.\n- We now move over to $t=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$. For $x$, we have\n$x|= t^{2}; x|= (-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}; = \\frac{9}{4}$.\nFor $y$, we have\n$y|= 2t; = 2(-\\frac{3}{2}); =-3$.\nSo the coordinates of the second intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$ at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right)\n$$"}]}
